          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How long does a common cold usually last?",
          "answer": "A common cold usually lasts about **7 to 10 days**. According to the general clinical reference on upper respiratory infection (common cold), symptoms usually peak around days two to three and then fade over the following week. Nasal congestion, sneezing, sore throat, mild headache, and sometimes a low-grade fever are typical. A cough often lasts longer, commonly two to three weeks, and this is normal.\n\nYellow or green nasal discharge is also part of the usual course. The antibiotic stewardship guidance states that this alone does not mean a bacterial infection, and that a cold lasting seven to ten days is running its normal course.\n\n**When to seek care:**\n- **Get emergency help right away** if you have severe trouble breathing, such as breathlessness at rest, being unable to speak in full sentences, or blue or gray lips or fingertips. The emergency breathing guidance also lists chest pain with breathlessness. Other warning signs of serious infection include confusion, unusual drowsiness, or a rash that does not fade when pressed. These are listed in the sepsis and meningitis guidance.\n- **Contact a clinician** if symptoms last ten days or more without any improvement. The stewardship guidance links this pattern to possible bacterial sinusitis, which needs a proper assessment.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not give a full list of other reasons to see a clinician during a cold, so a pharmacist or doctor is the right person to ask about your own situation.\n\n*Sources: Upper respiratory infection (common cold), general clinical reference; Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship guidance; Severe difficulty breathing, emergency medicine guidance; Sepsis and Signs suggesting meningitis, red-flag guidance.*\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
